[Article 3 of the Administrative Litigation Act [MT] (Types of Administrative Litigation)]
Administrative litigation is classified into the following four types:

(1) Appeal litigation: litigation filed against an administrative agency's disposition, etc., or omission;

(2) Party litigation: litigation concerning a legal relationship arising from an administrative agency's disposition, etc., and other litigation concerning public law legal relationships in which one of the parties to such legal relationship is the defendant;

(3) Public interest litigation: litigation filed by a person directly to seek rectification when an agency of the State or a public organization has committed an act contrary to law, regardless of his or her own legal interests;

(4) Institutional litigation: litigation filed when there is a dispute between agencies of the State or public organizations concerning the existence of authority or the exercise thereof; provided, that litigation that falls within the jurisdiction of the Constitutional Court under Article 2 of the Constitutional Court Act [MT] is excluded.
